          {
            "typeName": "publication",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "publicationCitation": {
                  "typeName": "publicationCitation",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Introduction: Urological complications after kidney transplantation are associated with significant morbidity, prolonged hospital stay and even mortality. The majority of urological complications are related to the ureteroneocystostomy, and the initial treatment for leakage or stenosis of the anastomosis is placement of a percutaneous nephrostomy (PCN) drain. Ureteral stents can minimize the number of urological complications, with two types of ureteral stents used: an internalized Double-J stent and an externalized Single-J stent. No well-designed prospective randomized controlled trial is available, as previous studies were retrospective in nature.\nMethods: We performed a single center randomized controlled trial. Patients were randomized to either an externalized Single-J stent, removed after 9 days, or an internal Double-J stent removed by cystoscopy after 3 weeks. Primary outcome was PCN placement within six months after transplantation, thereby assessing whether Double-J stenting was superior in preventing urological complications. Secondary outcomes encompassed urinary tract infections, cost-effectiveness, and hospital admission time.\nResults: The study was conducted from November 2018 to August 2023, during which 300 recipients were included with complete follow-up. PCN was performed in 14.5% in the Single-J group (21/145) and 4.5% in the Double-J group (7/155), p = 0.003. Multivariable logistic regression, corrected for recipient age, BMI, sex, and donor type, showed an OR of 0.26 [0.10, 0.61] (OR [95%CI]). All secondary outcomes were comparable, but hospital admission time and cost-effectiveness analysis favored Double-J stenting.\nDiscussion: This trial showed that Double-J stenting consistently reduced urological complications from 14.5% to 4.5%, while being highly cost-effective."
                }
              }
            ]
